<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Via Aemilia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">187 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Via Emilia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Via Aemilia was a Roman road built in 187 BCE by the consul Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, running in a straight line along the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> from Ariminum (Rimini) to Placentia (Piacenza) on the Po. Towns along it, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, Reggio and Parma, grew into the medieval communes of the region, which still bears its name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilia">Emilia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/via-aemilia.jpg" alt="Via Aemilia" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MacMoreno · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VIII_-_AEMILIA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
